      <h2 id="ffmi-research-reference-title" class="font-semibold">Research Reference — No Universal Pass/Fail Standard</h2>
      <ul class="mt-2 space-y-2 text-xs leading-5 text-blue-800 dark:text-blue-300">
        <li><strong>Clinical low-FFMI context:</strong> ESPEN uses values below 17 kg/m² for men and below 15 kg/m² for women only as part of a broader malnutrition assessment that also requires nutrition-risk screening and unintentional weight loss.</li>
        <li><strong>Healthy-adult research context:</strong> One Italian DXA study reported a middle-50% reference interval of 18.7–21.0 kg/m² for men and 14.9–17.2 kg/m² for women. These are population- and method-specific research values, not universal targets.</li>
        <li><strong>Athletic context:</strong> A normalized FFMI near 25 is a historical male-athlete research heuristic, not an official natural limit, health rating, or evidence of drug use.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-3 text-xs leading-5 text-blue-700/90 dark:text-blue-300/90">
        Compare results using the same body-fat method over time. For clinical interpretation, use professionally measured body composition and qualified medical or nutrition guidance.
      </p>
